# Analysing a Castor EDC study with Explorer

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:ex_castor_edc_explorer, "~> 0.1.0"},
  {:kino_explorer, "~> 0.1.25"}
])
```

## Introduction

This notebook loads a study's data into Explorer dataframes and takes a first look at it.
Every frame's dtypes come from the study's structure, not from the values it happens to hold.

## Connecting

You need a client ID and a client secret, which you can create on your [account settings page](https://data.castoredc.com/account/settings), and the ID of a study they have access to.

```elixir
regions = [
  {"https://data.castoredc.com", "NL"},
  {"https://uk.castoredc.com", "UK"},
  {"https://us.castoredc.com", "US"},
  {"https://au.castoredc.com", "AU"}
]

region = Kino.Input.select("Region", regions) |> Kino.render()
client_id = Kino.Input.text("Client ID") |> Kino.render()
client_secret = Kino.Input.password("Client secret") |> Kino.render()
study_id = Kino.Input.text("Study ID") |> Kino.render()

Kino.nothing()
```

Explorer's queries, such as `filter` and `summarise`, are macros, so the notebook requires `Explorer.DataFrame` once.

```elixir
require Explorer.DataFrame

client =
  CastorEDC.new(
    client_id: Kino.Input.read(client_id),
    client_secret: Kino.Input.read(client_secret),
    base_url: Kino.Input.read(region)
  )

study_id = Kino.Input.read(study_id)
```

## The study's structure

`fields/2` has a row per field that holds values, in the order the study shows them: where it collects data, its type, and its option group.

```elixir
{:ok, fields} = CastorEDC.Explorer.fields(client, study_id)
fields
```

`option_groups/2` has a row per option, to join on `option_group_id`.

```elixir
{:ok, options} = CastorEDC.Explorer.option_groups(client, study_id)
options
```

## Participants and sites

```elixir
{:ok, participants} = CastorEDC.Explorer.participants(client, study_id)
{:ok, sites} = CastorEDC.Explorer.sites(client, study_id)

participants
|> Explorer.DataFrame.join(Explorer.DataFrame.select(sites, ["site_id", "name"]), on: "site_id", how: :left)
|> Explorer.DataFrame.group_by("name")
|> Explorer.DataFrame.summarise(participants: count(participant_id), mean_progress: mean(progress))
```

## Collected data

`participant_data/3` runs a batch export and reads it: a frame for the study's values, with a row per participant, and one for each repeating data and survey, with a row per instance.
It takes a few requests, however big the study is.

```elixir
{:ok, %{study: study, repeating_data: repeating_data, surveys: surveys}} =
  CastorEDC.Explorer.participant_data(client, study_id, missing_columns: true)

study
```

Values marked missing are nulls, with their codes (95 to 99) in the `<variable>_missing` columns.

```elixir
Map.keys(repeating_data) ++ Map.keys(surveys)
```

`data_points/3` reads the same values through the API instead, page by page, with a row per value, which suits filtering and pivoting:

```elixir
{:ok, values} = CastorEDC.Explorer.data_points(client, study_id)

values
|> Explorer.DataFrame.filter(not is_nil(missing))
|> Explorer.DataFrame.group_by(["variable_name", "missing"])
|> Explorer.DataFrame.summarise(count: count(participant_id))
```

With `format: :wide`, it gives the same shape as `participant_data/3`, with a column per variable.
